 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Countable nouns can be counted and have a plural: one coin, two coins; a bank, three banks.\nUncountable nouns cannot be counted and have no plural: money, water, rice, bread, information, advice, furniture, luggage. We do not say “a money” or “informations”.\n• many / a few / few + countable plural: many coins, a few banks.\n• much / a little / little + uncountable: much money, a little water.\n• a lot of, some, any go with both: a lot of coins, a lot of money.\n• To count an uncountable noun, use a unit: a piece of advice, a bag of rice, a glass of water.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Correct: “She gave me many informations and two advices.”",
  "steps": [
   "information and advice are uncountable: no plural, no “many”.",
   "She gave me a lot of information and two pieces of advice."
  ],
  "answer": "a lot of information and two pieces of advice",
  "checks": []
 },
